{"seq":1245,"lane":"B","class":"wrong-narrower-question","severity":"serious","evidence":"docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1245.json, e2: \"CEO Jensen Huang has told investors he expects $1 trillion in combined Blackwell and Rubin chip sales between 2025 and the end of 2027\"; linked to v2.","rule":"Signed standard §5.1 and D207: file helpful evidence under the narrowest existing question it bears on. v10 asks about Data Center revenue; v2 asks about total revenue.","mechanism":"B files a data-center product sales forecast under the broader total-revenue question. The D273 fallback retained by D361 permits the broad revenue destination."}
{"seq":1246,"lane":"B","class":"wrong-narrower-question","severity":"serious","evidence":"docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1246.json, e7: \"Management continues to see around $20 billion in total server CPU demand and preliminarily expects CPU revenue to more than double in fiscal 2028.\"; linked only to v72.","rule":"Signed standard §5.1 and D207: file helpful evidence under the narrowest existing question it bears on. v7 tracks stand-alone CPU revenue; v72 tracks total FY2028 revenue.","mechanism":"B sends a CPU-specific demand and revenue outlook to total revenue despite the existing CPU question. The broad fallback can attract evidence away from a narrower question."}
{"seq":1246,"lane":"B","class":"missed-existing-evidence","severity":"serious","evidence":"docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1246.json, article: \"the next-generation Vera CPU was already in full production at the end of the second quarter.\" B does not include this statement.","rule":"Signed standard §5.1: retain a concrete product or supply development an analyst would note when it can make an existing question's answer more or less likely; v7 tracks stand-alone CPU revenue.","mechanism":"B captured the adjacent trailing CPU revenue and demand forecast but omitted the production status of the next-generation CPU. The trace does not establish which request instruction caused the omission."}
{"seq":1249,"lane":"B","class":"wrong-narrower-question","severity":"serious","evidence":"docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1249.json, e4: \"Nvidia still controls roughly three-quarters of global AI accelerator revenue, even after its share has fallen from a peak near 87% as AMD and hyperscaler custom chips start to nibble at the edges.\"; linked to v2.","rule":"Signed standard §5.1 and D207: file evidence under the narrowest existing question it bears on. v10 tracks Data Center revenue, while v2 tracks total revenue.","mechanism":"B places competition and share evidence for the data-center accelerator business under total revenue. The fallback can select the broader question."}
{"seq":1249,"lane":"judging","class":"lower-bound-treated-as-point","severity":"serious","evidence":"docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1249.json, v80: \"The reference is the article’s forecast of more than $200 billion, corresponding to rung 3’s $200 billion–below $250 billion band.\" The cited article says \"more than $200 billion\" and gives no upper bound.","rule":"Signed standard §5.4 requires preserving a statement's limits; §7 and D218 require the judgement to be supported by the evidence.","mechanism":"Record preparation uses an open-ended floor as the comparison for a bounded middle range, and judging selects that range despite acknowledging that the source has no upper bound."}
{"seq":1249,"lane":"B","class":"same-article-evidence-duplicated","severity":"serious","evidence":"docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1249.json and _ledger.json: \"The AI accelerator market is expected to grow from more than $200 billion in 2026 to over $430 billion by 2035.\" was written as e4338 under v80 and e4345 under v72.","rule":"Signed standard §2 and D250: one research matter in one article is one evidence record; D207 calls for the narrowest home.","mechanism":"A and B read the same pre-item library in parallel. build_handover appends their passages separately, so B cannot see the new question A opens and the writer stores the same passage twice."}
{"seq":1250,"lane":"A","class":"duplicate-aggregate-proposal","severity":"minor","evidence":"docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1250.json, c1: \"How much will the five top hyperscalers spend on capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\" Existing v5 asks how much the four largest AI hyperscalers will spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027. Matching merged c1 into v5.","rule":"Signed standard §§3 and 4 and D197: another population or proxy of the same shared aggregate in the same year is evidence for the existing aggregate question.","mechanism":"The A request permits a separate question when another outcome could change independently, but does not state the specific rule treating another population of the same aggregate as evidence. Matching applies the shared-quantity key and prevents a second question from being written."}
{"seq":1250,"lane":"matching","class":"same-article-evidence-duplicated","severity":"serious","evidence":"docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1250.json and _ledger.json: \"On the Q2 2027 earnings call, Chief Financial Officer Colette Kress mentioned that the five top hyperscalers will spend $1.3 trillion on capital expenditures in 2027.\" was written as both e4346 and e4350 under v5.","rule":"Signed standard §2 and D250: one research matter in one article is one evidence record.","mechanism":"The merged A passage and B passage enter separate handover records. Matching marks both as repeats of earlier evidence but the writer still stores both copies from this same article."}
{"seq":1251,"lane":"B","class":"same-article-evidence-duplicated","severity":"serious","evidence":"docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1251.json and _ledger.json: \"According to the latest guidance from chief financial officer Colette Kress, around 25% of Nvidia's sales in fiscal 2028 will come from customers it has financed in some way.\" was written as e4351 under v81 and e4359 under v72.","rule":"Signed standard §2 and D250: one research matter in one article is one evidence record; D207 calls for the narrowest home.","mechanism":"A opens v81 while B independently files the same passage under the existing total-revenue question. The handover retains both records."}
{"seq":1251,"lane":"judging","class":"financing-amount-misinterpreted","severity":"serious","evidence":"docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1251.json, v81 judgement: \"the $168 billion estimate is explicitly conditional on revenue exceeding $670 billion and is consistent with roughly 25%\"; the article says \"around $168 billion could be funded by its own balance sheet,\" while the 25% figure is sales from customers NVIDIA has financed in some way.","rule":"Signed standard §§5.4 and 7: preserve what a figure measures and distinguish an attributed inference from what the cited evidence establishes.","mechanism":"The judgement treats the author's estimated amount funded as supporting the share of revenue from financed customers. A customer's revenue and the amount NVIDIA finances are different quantities; the cited calculation does not establish that they are equal."}
{"seq":1252,"lane":"A","class":"missing-product-question","severity":"serious","evidence":"docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1252.json, article: \"CEO Elon Musk said early this year that the electric vehicle company could be mass-manufacturing and selling its humanoid assistants by the end of 2027\" and could \"eventually account for the vast majority of Tesla's market value.\" A proposes no Tesla product question.","rule":"Signed standard §§3 B and 4: a principal product milestone that could change an investment decision and can be checked publicly may open when no existing question covers it. D155 says companies outside the initial list cannot be ignored.","mechanism":"A proposes an NVIDIA market-share question but misses the separately stated Tesla commercialization milestone. The trace does not establish the cause of the omission."}
{"seq":1252,"lane":"B","class":"lost-uncertainty","severity":"serious","evidence":"docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1252.json, article: \"I'm betting that many of these institutions are already shopping around for other platforms.\" B's e7 summary says \"poor AI returns on investment are prompting many AI-using institutions to shop for alternative platforms.\"","rule":"Signed standard §5.4: preserve the original statement's uncertainty and distinguish an author's view from an established event.","mechanism":"B converts the author's speculation into an asserted current customer action. The A summary of the same passage correctly says it is not confirmed switching."}
{"seq":1252,"lane":"B","class":"same-article-evidence-duplicated","severity":"serious","evidence":"docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/traces/split-v2-confirm-first100-2026-11-05/1252.json and _ledger.json: A's e4361 and e4362 and B's e4366 repeat the article's account that \"alternatives are quickly coming into the mix\" and that the cloud-provider chips were \"built from the ground up as alternatives to Nvidia's expensive hardware.\"","rule":"Signed standard §2 and D250: one research matter in one article is one evidence record; D207 calls for the narrowest home.","mechanism":"A and B independently extract overlapping passages about the same competitive development, and build_handover appends them without reconciling the article-level matter."}
{"summary":{"items_read":[1245,1246,1247,1248,1249,1250,1251,1252],"classes":{"wrong-narrower-question":3,"missed-existing-evidence":1,"lower-bound-treated-as-point":1,"same-article-evidence-duplicated":4,"duplicate-aggregate-proposal":1,"financing-amount-misinterpreted":1,"missing-product-question":1,"lost-uncertainty":1}}}